      <item>
         <title><![CDATA[Canada looking to Germany for 8 submarines up front and 4 later: sources]]></title>
         <link>https://www.cbc.ca/news/politics/canada-germany-submarines-defence-industry-economy-9.7407952?cmp=rss</link>
         <description><![CDATA[<img src='https://i.cbc.ca/ais/b4aa780a-98f1-4b79-bbe6-04208f35cbda,1793749608585/full/max/0/default.jpg?im=Crop%2Crect%3D%280%2C114%2C8155%2C4587%29%3B' alt='three men hold a model submarine' width='620' height='349' title='Prime Minister Mark Carney, right, German Chancellor Friedrich Merz, centre, and Norwegian Prime Minister Jonas Gahr Store hold a small model of a TKMS submarine before meeting in Ankara, Turkey, Tuesday, July 7, 2026. THE CANADIAN PRESS/Adrian Wyld'/><p>Canada wants to split its planned purchase of 12 German submarines, committing to eight up front while leaving the remaining four for a future decision. Sources tell CBC News the proposal is intended to preserve financial flexibility but could jeopardize some of the $86 billion in economic benefits promised by Germany.</p>]]></description>
         <pubDate>Wed, 04 Nov 2026 04:00:00 EST</pubDate>
         <category>News/Politics</category>
         <guid isPermaLink="false">9.7407952</guid>
         <dc:creator>Murray Brewster, Daniel Leblanc</dc:creator>
         <dc:type>story</dc:type>
         <dc:rights>Syndication Allowed</dc:rights>
      </item>
