      <item>
         <title><![CDATA[A runner died in the New York City Marathon. Have the risks changed as more people race?]]></title>
         <link>https://www.cbc.ca/news/world/woman-dies-new-york-marathon-risks-9.7406942?cmp=rss</link>
         <description><![CDATA[<img src='https://i.cbc.ca/ais/db140a1e-c3b3-48d7-887d-f59f43fdcd05,1793713977873/full/max/0/default.jpg?im=Crop%2Crect%3D%280%2C504%2C4849%2C2727%29%3B' alt='A mass of runners going across a bridge' width='620' height='349' title='Runners cross the Verrazzano-Narrows Bridge during the New York City Marathon in New York, Sunday, Nov. 1, 2026.'/><p>Every year, amid our current running boom, more and more people vie for the chance to lace up their sneakers and push themselves 42.2 kilometres to cross that finish line. But every year, young and seemingly healthy people also die trying.</p>]]></description>
